  "editorial": {
    "status": "working-edition",
    "notes": "The Last Gospel: the greeting at the Gospel corner, the announcement of the beginning of St John with its answer, John 1, 1-14, the genuflection at Et Verbum caro factum est, and the closing Deo grátias. The largest single text in the corpus. VENIT at w077 and w127 is perfect, not present: the two are spelled alike, and the narrative around them decides — Fuit at w067, erat at w074, recepérunt at w132. PLENUM at w184 is neuter nominative and refers back to VERBUM at w167, across the intervening description of the glory beheld; its genitives are GRÁTIÆ at w185 and VERITÁTIS at w187. The reader note cites the same construction in the Glossa ordinaria. NIHIL at w044 is indeclinable: the corpus states the syntactic case, number and gender the sentence gives it, though the form itself marks none. QUOD at w045 opens a clause that witness do, the hand missal and mr all point as belonging to what precedes (vg-clementina's substantive-only profile leaves its punctuation out of scope); the corpus follows their punctuation, which is the whole of the decision. QUASI at w180 is tagged conj, as sicut is under the classification ruling recorded in SCHEMA.md. NEQUE at w153 and w157 has its own lemma beside nec: Collatinus heads neque itself and would not link the form to nec. SUI at w129 (suus, the reflexive possessive) and PRÓPRIA at w126 (proprius, an adjective of ownership) are two different words in one and the same use — substantival, without a noun: neuter plural for his own things, masculine plural for his own people. The auxiliaries at w038, w043, w047, w119, w165 and w170 are separate tokens of sum; the participle beside each carries the tense. FACTA at w037 and FACTUS at w118 name Whitaker's alone; FÍERI at w142 rests on the recorded ruling about fio's infinitive. Witness do does not print the two responses in its Ordo chain — it keeps each once as a shared block and calls it from many places — and the witness file declares that expansion.",
